From 9c6f7aa4f4c2c85d837c5389bbfd41f5e222fa0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CNY Healthcare <cnyhealthymall@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 16:46:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] fix(client): put every LINE fetch under a deadline that
 covers the body read
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two separate gaps in LineClient.

request() armed an AbortController but cleared it in a `finally` that ran the
moment the Response object arrived — before `response.text()`. Headers can
land in milliseconds while the body trickles or never finishes, so the body
read had no ceiling at all and a stalled peer could hang an MCP tool call
indefinitely. The timer now stays armed until the body is read, and an abort
during that read is wrapped in the same LineApiError shape as any other
transport failure instead of escaping as a raw AbortError.

uploadRichMenuImage() and verifyAccessToken() bypass request() entirely (raw
binary body on api-data; access_token as a query param, no Bearer) and so
carried no timeout of any kind. Both now run under their own deadline —
60s for the upload, which pushes up to 1MB, and 30s for the verify. Timeouts
move to named constants rather than a literal in the middle of request().

Tests: a new suite drives each path with faked timers and a promise that
settles only via its AbortSignal — proving the stall is cut at the deadline,
that the surfaced error is a LineApiError, that a LINE error status still
reports its own status rather than a timeout, and that no call leaves a
dangling timer behind.
